    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d439">

                <pb facs="0619" n="529" xml:id="pg529"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">832.85/117: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Wallace</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-03-20T16:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">March 20,
                                1920—4 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 4:20 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>763. R–438 for the Department and Davis. Reference your 517 March 12, 4 p.m.</p>
                <p>First. Maritime Service in London adopted March 4th, resolutions postponing
                    consideration Brazilian ship question until arrival of Brazilian
                        delegates.<anchor n="36" xml:id="d439-f1"/> Rathbone.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wallace</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d439-f1" n="36" xml:id="d439-f1-content">
                <p>The Ambassador in Brazil reported, Dec. 1, 1920: “Negotiations between France and
                    Brazil regarding German steamers seized in Brazilian ports are concluded on the
                    basis of following principles. 1. The vessels now recognized as the permanent
                    property of Brazil; 2. The arrangements will be based on article 297 of the
                    Treaty of Versailles.” (File no. 832.85/124.)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
